  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why \"pure\" gold is labeled as 99.9% (or 999 fineness) rather than 100%:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c It's Physically Impossible to Achieve 100% Purity\n\nGold that is 100% pure is nearly impossible to achieve. True 100% purity would mean that a sample contains **only** gold atoms \u2014 perfect purity would mean the piece has zero non-gold atoms, but nature has its own plans, and pure gold naturally has some other atoms mingling in it.\n\n---\n\n### \u2697\ufe0f Limitations of the Refining Process\n\nIt is currently impossible to refine gold to a purity of 100% \u2014 1,000 fineness. No matter how advanced refining techniques become, refining metals to remove impurities costs money, and there's no benefit in making gold bullion any better than 99.99%, as doing so just raises the cost with no added benefit.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddea Trace Impurities Are Unavoidable\n\nPure gold naturally has some other atoms mingling in it, and these so-called \"impurities\" actually have a job \u2014 they help stabilize the metal's structure. They are a small amount, but it makes a world of difference to the end product. In fact, taking out all the impurities might make the metal not act like the gold we know and love, as these extra elements give it its charm, like a beautiful color, flexibility, and the ability to stay shiny without tarnishing.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccf How Purity is Measured\n\nGold's purity is graded based on the **millesimal fineness scale**, which is used to determine the purity of a precious metal by measuring the number of parts per thousand of pure metal. Typically, anything that is 99.9% gold (999) or more is considered fine gold. The purest gold is 999.99, also called \"five nines\" fine or \"five nines\" pure. Just below this is 999.9 gold, which is also called \"four nines\" fine or \"four nines\" pure.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc5 The Closest We've Ever Gotten\n\n999.999 fineness is the purest gold ever produced, and it proved too expensive to be a regular occurrence. Generally, the closest to 100% pure gold that refiners are able to reach in everyday production is a fineness of 999.9 gold. The British Royal Mint, the Royal Canadian Mint, and the Perth Mint all strike their gold coins to this purity of 999.9 gold, making it one of the most commonly seen purities for bullion coins and bars.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 In Summary\n\nThe 99.9% (999) label on \"pure\" gold isn't a marketing shortcut \u2014 it reflects a genuine physical and technological limitation. Both 24 karat and 999.9 fineness are generally accepted as pure gold. Despite this, in truth it is impossible to produce 100% pure gold based on current technical limits.",
